    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the family court act and the domestic relations law, in relation to service of orders of protection including applications to extend and petitions for violations of orders of protection and temporary orders of protection in family court",
    "basePrintNo": "S8058",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8058-2009",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §153-b, Fam Ct Act; amd §240, Dom Rel L",
    "lawSection": "Family Court Act",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S8058\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act\nto amend the family court act and the domestic relations law, in\nrelation to service of orders of protection including applications to\nextend and petitions for violations of orders of protection and\ntemporary orders of protection in family court\n\nThis is one in a series of measures being introduced at the request of\nthe Chief Administrative Judge upon the recommendation of her Family\nCourt Advisory and Rules Committee.\n\nSection 153-b of the Family Court Act, enacted in 1994 and amended in\n1995, provided important assistance to litigants seeking temporary\nand final orders of protection by permitting them the option of\nhaving peace and police officers serve the orders and accompanying\npleadings. See L. 1994, c. 46; L. 1995, c.429. These litigants are\noften in the midst of serious emergencies, may be seeking protection\nfrom dangerous situations, and have neither the wherewithal, nor the\neconomic resources, to arrange for service by a non-party over the\nage of 18, as required by section 2103(a) of the Civil Practice Law\nand Rules. Many are not represented by counsel, at least at the early\nstages of the litigation. In fact, it has been estimated that 75% of\nthe total caseload of the New York City Family Court involves\nself-represented litigants. Since orders of protection are not\nenforceable until served, this assistance is critical to victim\nsafety, as well as to\nadvancing the litigation and assuming jurisdiction over the alleged\nabuser. Coupled with Family Court Act §153-c, which provides\nlitigants with quick access to the court for the purposes of\nobtaining temporary relief, service of pleadings and orders by law\nenforcement has been critical to the effectiveness of the Family\nCourt's response to cases of domestic violence.\n\nThis assistance was intended for all stages of Family Court\nproceedings regarding orders of protection, specifically when orders\nare extended and when petitions alleging violations are filed.\nHowever, there are reports that law enforcement are declining to\nserve orders of protection or temporary orders of protection issued\nin later stages of proceedings, such as violations. Again, the nature\nof the litigation dictates the need for concerns for victim safety,\nas litigants may be in situations of acute danger. If they elect to\nprosecute violations criminally, they would necessarily have the\nbenefit of law enforcement assistance and involvement. The fact that\nthey may elect to prosecute civilly instead should not deprive them\nof needed assistance in serving the papers. Family Court Act §846(c),\nincorporating Family Court Act §826 by reference, requires attempted\npersonal service prior to substituted service being ordered.\nAdditionally, extended orders, like initial orders of protection, are\n\nnot enforceable until served and if in personam jurisdiction had not\nyet been obtained over the respondent, personal service may be\nnecessary. The Family Court Advisory and Rules Committee, therefore,\nis submitting a proposal that clarifies that litigants\nhave the same options of peace and police officer service for\norders and temporary orders and legal papers issued in later stages\nof family offense proceedings including service of extended orders\nand for petitions alleging violations of orders of protection as they\nhave for original orders and accompanying pleadings.\n\nThe proposal also makes it clear that such service must be available\nwithout fee to the litigants. The provision prohibiting fees from\nbeing charged by police and peace officers for service of all orders\nof protection, whether issued upon default, temporary, modified,\nfinal, or extended as well as associated petitions and summonses,\nemphasizes legislation enacted in 2007 at the recommendation of the\nNew York State Office for the Prevention of Domestic Violence. That\nstatute amended section 8011 of the Civil Practice Law and Rules to\nprohibit sheriffs from charging the statutory $45 fee and any mileage\nfees for service of orders of protection and related orders or papers\nwhen service has\nbeen directed by the court. See L. 2007, c. 36. Recognizing that\nvictims of domestic violence are charged no fees for service of\ncriminal orders of protection and related documents, the statute was\nintended to ensure compliance a statewide policy and practice that\nwould create equity between criminal and civil options, support\nsafety for domestic violence victims, and be consistent with New\nYork's proactive, aggressive response to domestic violence.\nMcKinney's 2007 Session Laws of New York, c. 36, New York State\nMemorandum in Support of Senate bill S4020, p. 1560. Like the 2007\namendment, the Committee's proposal is essential to ensure compliance\nby New York State with Federal law. The Federal Violence Against\nWomen Act requires all states in receipt of Federal funding under the\nSTOP Formula Grant Program under the Act to:\n\ncertify that their laws, policies, and practices do not require, in\nconnection with the prosecution of any misdemeanor or felony domestic\nviolence offense, or in connection with the filing, issuance,\nregistration, or service of a protection order, or a petition for a\nprotection order, to protect a victim of domestic violence, stalking,\nor sexual assault, that the victim bear the costs associated with the\nfiling of criminal charges against the offender, or the costs\nassociated with the filing, issuance, registration, or service of a\nwarrant, protection order, petition for a protection order, or\nwitness subpoena, whether issued inside or outside the State, tribal,\nor local jurisdiction[.]\n42 U.S.C.A. §3796hh(c)(4).\n\nLifting the burden from victims of domestic violence of arranging for\nservice of orders of protection has proven to be essential to the\neffort to make civil prosecution in Family Court an effective avenue\nof relief. Clarification that this same opportunity for law\n\nenforcement service with respect to all orders and petitions for\nviolations of orders, is equally critical to that end.\n\nThis measure, which would have no fiscal impact upon the State, would\ntake effect immediately.\n\n2010 LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNone. New proposal.\n\n",
